 \nL907: ﬁle table.m posted on his web site and containing bases of various spaces of colored diagrams.\nL908: \nL909: However, the preprint [3], which contains comments on this table, indicates that the program used\nL910: to obtain the table is not ready for publication owing to its awkwardness and some drawbacks in\nL911: \nL912: the algorithm.\nL913: \nL914: Remark 2. Bar-Natan’s table also shows that 7 is the smallest degree of a Vassiliev invariant\nL915: \nL916: that can detect the orientation of two-component string links. The computations required to check\nL917: \nL918: this fact can easily be done by hand.\nL919: \nL920: 6. Deframing\nL921: \nL922: As was mentioned in the introduction, the proofs given in Secs. 3 and 5 actually refer to the case\nL923: of framed links, because 1-term relations were not taken into consideration. Generally speaking,\nL924: \nL925: detecting the orientation of a framed link is easier, because it contains an additional structure,\nL926: \nL927: which, in principle, may not be preserved by the inversion. In this section, we prove that the main\nL928: \nL929: theorem remains valid for conventional (unframed) links.\nL930: \nL931: Indeed, let A(2) be the quotient of the space A(2) modulo 1-term relations, i.e., by the ideal\nL932: generated by the diagrams a 1(with 1 chord on the ﬁrst component of the support) and a 2(with\nL933: \nL934: 1 chord on the second component of the support): A(2) = A(2)/a 1,a\nL935: \nL936: 2, where angle brackets\nL937: \nL938: denote the 2-sided ideal with given generators. The quotient algebra A(2) can also be considered\nL939: \nL940: as a subalgebra of A(2): by the structure theorem for cocommutative Hopf algebras, A(2) is the\nL941: \nL942: universal enveloping algebra of the Lie algebra P of its primitive elements P,soifwetakethe Q1\nL943: subspace of P spanned by all connected diagrams except for a 1 and a 2, we obtain an embedding\nL944: \nL945: A(2) ⊂A(2). Since the diagrams displayed in Proposition 1 belong to A(2), this means that\nL946: \nL947: noninvertibility holds also for unframed string links.\nL948: \nL949: Now let us show that the second proof (Sec. 5) is valid in the unframed case as well. Let\nL950: \nL951: χ:A(2) →B(2) be the isomorphism deﬁned above.\nL952: \nL953: Lemma 8. The subspace χ−1(A(2)) = B(2) coincides with the subalgebra of B(2) generated\nL954: by all connected Jacobi diagrams except for b 1=11\nL955: \nL956: and b 2=22\nL957: \nL958: .\nL959: \nL960: Proof. The assertion is nontrivial, because χ does not preserve the multiplication and the\nL961: \nL962: subalgebras A(2) and B(2) are described by their generators in the sense of diﬀerent multiplica-\nL963: \nL964: tions. However, it is easily seen by a straightforward argument that the image of a Jacobi diagram\nL965: \nL966: diﬀerent from b 1 and b 2 under χ is a linear combination of products of connected chord diagrams\nL967: \nL968: diﬀerent from a 1 and a 2.\nL982: Comment. Should such a mapping exist, one could try to apply the technique of this paper\nL983: \nL984: to the invertibility problem for knots. Unfortunately, it is easily seen that the standard doubling\nL985: \nL986: operator ∆: B(1) →B(2) deﬁned in [1] and [12] is no good to this end.\nL987: \nL988: Question 2. Do Vassiliev invariants detect invertibility of closed 2-component links?\nL989: \nL993: 2) of chord diagrams on two circles. There is an obvious epimorphism A(R 1\nL994: \nL995: 2)→A(S 1\nL996: \nL997: 2),\nL998: \nL999: whose kernel is spanned by the so called link relations (see [4]). To solve the problem, one should\nL1000: \nL1001: study the interplay between link relations and orientation reversal.\nL1002: \nL1003: Acknowledgements\nL1004: \nL1005: We are obliged A. Khoroshkin, S. Loktev and E. B. Vinberg for useful comments on invariant\nL1006: \nL1007: theory. We thank S. Lando and the referee, who read the text and made a number of useful remarks.\nL1008: \nL1009: References\nL1010: [1] D. Bar-Natan, “On the Vassiliev knot invariants,” Topology, 34:2 (1995), 423–472.\nL1011: \nL1012: [2] D. Bar-Natan, “Vassiliev homotopy string link invariants,” J. Knot Theory Ramiﬁcations, 4:1\nL1013: \nL1014: (1995), 13–32.\nL1015: \nL1016: [3] D. Bar-Natan, Some computations related to Vassiliev invariants, http://www.math.\nL1017: \nL1018: toronto.edu/~drorbn/papers, 1996.\nL1019: \nL1020: [4] D. Bar-Natan, S. Garoufalidis, L. Rozansky, and D. Thurston, “The ˚\nL1021: \nL1022: Arhus integral of rational\nL1023: homology 3-spheres II: Invariance and universality,” Selecta Math. (N. S.), 8:3 (2002), 341–371\nL1024: \nL1025: .\nL1026: \nL1027: [5] S. Duzhin, Computer programs and data ﬁles for the calculation of the weight systems ϕ and\nL1028: \nL1029: ψ,http://www.pdmi.ras.ru/~arnsem/dataprog/OrLinks/.\nL1030: \nL1031: [6] S. Chmutov, S. Duzhin, and J. Mostovoy, CDBook. Introduction to Vassiliev knot invariants,\nL1032: \nL1033: draft version of a book, Online at http://www.pdmi.ras.ru/~duzhin/papers/.\nL1257: 【30†View】\nL1258: \nL1259: Show abstract\nL1260: \nL1261: Show more\nL1262: \nL1263: 【33†Invariant theory】\nL1264: \nL1265: Article\nL1266: \nL1267: Full-text available\nL1268: \nL1269:   * Jan 1989\nL1270: \nL1271:   * 【34†Vladimir L. Popov】\nL1272:   * 【35†E. B. Vinberg】\nL1273: \nL1274: 【33†View】\nL1275: \nL1276: 【36†Vassiliev’s knot invariants】\nL1277: \nL1278: Chapter\nL1279: \nL1280:   * Aug 1993\nL1281: \nL1282:   * 【37†M. Kontsevich】\nL1283: \nL1284: 【36†View】\nL1285: \nL1286: 【38†Vassiliev’s knot invariants】\nL1287: \nL1288: Article\nL1289: \nL1290:   * 【39†Maxim Kontsevich】\nL1291: \nL1292: 【38†View】\nL1293: \nL1294: 【40†The invertibility problem on amphicheiral excellent knots】\nL1295: \nL1296: Article\nL1297: \nL1298:   * Jan 1979\nL1299:   * P JPN ACAD A-MATH\nL1300: \nL1301:   * 【41†Akio Kawauchi】\nL1302: \nL1303: 【40†View】\nL1304: 【42†Vassiliev homotopy string link invariants】\nL1305: \nL1306: Article\nL1307: \nL1308:   * Mar 1995\nL1309: \nL1310:   * 【43†Dror Bar-Natan】\nL1311: We investigate Vassiliev homotopy invariants of string links, and find that in this particular case, most of the questions left unanswered in (3) can be answered ar- matively. In particular, Vassiliev invariants classify string links up to homotopy, and all Vassiliev homotopy string link invariants come from marked surfaces as in (3), using the same construction that in the case of knots gives the HOMFLY and Kauman polynomials.\nL1312: Alongside, the Milnor \" invariants of string links are shown to be Vassiliev invariants, and it is re-proven, by elementary means, that Vassiliev invariants classify braids.\nL1313: 【42†View】\nL1314: \nL1315: Show abstract\nL1316: \nL1317: 【44†Non-invertible knots exist】\nL1318: \nL1319: Article\nL1320: \nL1321:   * Dec 1963\nL1322: \nL1323:   * 【45†H.F. Trotter】\nL1324: \nL1325: 【44†View】\nL1326: \nL1327: 【46†On the Vassiliev Knot Invariants】\nL1328: \nL1329: Article\nL1330: \nL1331:   * Apr 1995\nL1332: \nL1333:   * 【47†D. Bar Natan】\nL1334: \nL1335: 【46†View】\nL1336: \nL1337: 【48†SOME COMPUTATIONS RELATED TO VASSILIEV INVARIANTS】\nL1338: \nL1339: Article\nL1340: \nL1341:   * Jan 1996\nL1342: \nL1343:   * 【43†Dror Bar-Natan】\nL1344: This is a regularly updated collection of results of computations related to Vassiliev invariants. It is distributed using photocopiers, laser printers, and the internet. If you have data to add, please let me know!\nL1345: \nL1346: 【48†View】\nL1347: \nL1348: Show abstract\nL1349: \nL1350: 【49†The Classical Groups | Their Invariants and Representations】\nL1351: \nL1352: Book\nL1353: \nL1354:   * Jan 1946\nL1355: \nL1356:   * 【50†HERMANN WEYL】\nL1357: \nL1358: 【49†View】\nL1359: \nL1360: 【51†Finite-type invariants of knots, links, and graphs】\nL1361: \nL1362: Article\nL1363: \nL1364:   * Oct 1996\nL1365: \nL1366:   * 【52†Ted Stanford】\nL1367: We define finite-type invariants for graphs as functionals on certain finite-dimensional vector spaces generated by spatial graphs. These invariants are generalizations of Vassiliev's knot invariants to links and graphs, but our methods are quite different from those used in his paper. We show how to calculate finite-type invariants.\nL1368: In the case of links, we show a way to relate the invariants on links with different numbers of components, and we generalize a theorem of Birman and Lin to show that the Jones, HOMFLY, and Kauffman polynomials can be interpreted as sequences of finite-type invariants.\n"
